// `base.` runs the visibility check, so a derived type cannot reach a PRIVATE member of its base by
// choosing the other spelling.
//
// This used to compile, run, and return 42 while the same call written `this.secret()` was correctly
// rejected ("'secret' is private in 'B'") — the base path skipped `canAccess` entirely. Not
// memory-unsafe; it broke an encapsulation guarantee the language otherwise enforces.
//
// The fix applies the EXISTING rule rather than adding one: `_currentClass` is the derived type at a
// `base.` call, so `canAccess` walks its base chain and admits protected while rejecting private.
//
// ⚠️ `base.` is NOT restricted to protected-only. An earlier draft proposed that, and it is wrong on two
// counts: a derived type may legitimately call `base.<protected>` from a PUBLIC method, so the
// restriction would be on the wrong axis; and a `resource` field is always private while inheritance is
// resource-only, so there are no protected FIELDS to reach — this is entirely about methods.
// tests/vtable_depth2 covers the legal `base.<protected>` direction.
type virtual resource B {
    public ctor make() { }
    fn int32 secret() { return 42; }                  // no modifier -> private to B
    public fn int32 peek() { return this.peekImpl(); }
    protected virtual fn int32 peekImpl() { return 0; }
    protected virtual fn int32 t() { return 1; }
}
type final resource D extends B {
    public ctor make() { this.base = Base.make(); }
    protected override fn int32 t() { return 2; }
    protected override fn int32 peekImpl() { return base.secret(); }   // ERROR: 'secret' is private in 'B'
}
fn int main() { D d = D.make(); return d.peek(); }
